     "q": "Which crust is denser and can sink?",
     "options": [
      "Continental",
      "Oceanic",
      "Both equally"
     ],
     "a": "B) Oceanic. Oceanic crust (basalt) is denser, so it sinks beneath continental crust.",
     "marks": 1
    },
    {
     "q": "Which boundary has earthquakes but no volcanoes?",
     "options": [
      "Constructive",
      "Destructive",
      "Conservative"
     ],
     "a": "C) Conservative. At conservative boundaries plates slide past; no magma rises, so no volcanoes.",
     "marks": 1
    },
    {
     "q": "The point on the surface above the focus is the:",
     "options": [
      "Fault",
      "Epicentre",
      "Magnitude"
     ],
     "a": "B) Epicentre. The epicentre is directly above the focus, where shaking is usually strongest.",
     "marks": 1
    },
    {
     "q": "Tsunami waves grow tallest:",
     "options": [
      "In the deep ocean",
      "As they reach shallow water near the coast",
      "On land far inland"
     ],
     "a": "B) As they reach shallow water near the coast. Waves slow and pile up in shallow water, growing much taller.",
     "marks": 1
    },
    {
     "q": "Which can scientists predict better?",
     "options": [
      "Earthquakes",
      "Volcanic eruptions",
      "Both exactly"
     ],
     "a": "B) Volcanic eruptions. Volcanoes show warning signs such as gas and swelling; earthquakes cannot be predicted precisely.",
     "marks": 1
